.Dd June 4, 2026
.Dt LOADER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm loader
.Nd kernel bootstrapping final stage
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The program called
.Nm
is the final stage of
.Fx Ns 's
kernel bootstrapping process.
It is responsible for bringing the kernel, kernel modules and other files into
memory.
It creates a set of
.Xr sh 1
like environment variables that are passed to the kernel.
It executes boot scripts written in one of several interpreters.
Together with the scripts, it controls the booting process and
interaction with the user.
.Pp
It provides a scripting language that can be used to
automate tasks, do pre-configuration or assist in recovery
procedures.
This scripting language is roughly divided in
two main components.
The smaller one is a set of commands
designed for direct use by the casual user, called "builtin
commands" for historical reasons.
The main drive behind these commands is user-friendliness.
The larger component is the scripting language built into
the boot loader.
.Fx
provides three different interpreters: Forth, Lua and Simple.
The Forth loader is based on an ANS Forth compatible
Forth interpreter based on FICL, by
.An John Sadler .
The Lua loader is a full Lua interpreter from
.Pa https://www.lua.org/ .
The Simple loader only interprets a list of builtin commands
without any control structure.
.Pp
During initialization,
.Nm
will probe for a console and set the
.Va console
variable, or set it to serial console
.Pq Dq Li comconsole
if the previous boot stage used that.
If multiple consoles are selected, they will be listed separated by spaces.
Then, devices are probed,
.Va currdev
and
.Va loaddev
are set, and
.Va LINES
is set to 24.
Finally, an interpreter specific file will be executed.
.Sh BUILTIN COMMANDS
The commands common to all interpreters are described in the
.Xr loader_simp 8
.Dq BUILTIN COMMANDS
section.
.Pp
The following commands are only available in
.Xr loader_lua 8
and
.Xr loader_4th 8 :
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.\" sort the following entries according to the second field
.It Ic be-list
Lists the boot environments that are visible to
.Nm .
The listed names may be used directly with
.Ic be-switch .
.It Ic be-switch Ar beName
Switch to the
.Ar beName
boot environment.
The
.Nm
configuration will be reloaded from the new root, and any previously loaded
kernel and modules will be immediately unloaded.
.It Ic boot-conf
Load the
.Nm
config and commence the autoboot sequence.
.Pp
.It Ic read-conf Ar file
Load the specified configuration file.
.Pp
.It Ic reload-conf
Revert any previously applied settings, and reload the configuration.
Set comands that were executed at the command line to override variables
previously
.Cm set
by
.Xr loader.conf 5
configuration will also be reverted,
along with any module options that were toggled.
.Pp
.It Ic enable-module Ar kmod-name
.It Ic disable-module Ar kmod-name
.It Ic toggle-module Ar kmod-name
Enable, disable, or toggle loading of the kernel module named
.Dq Ar kmod-name .
.Pp
.It Ic show-module-options
Describe all modules the
.Nm
is aware of, and show if they are enabled or not.
.El
.Pp
The following command is only available in
.Xr loader_lua 8 :
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Ic disable-device Ar device
Set a newbus hint to disable the
.Ar device .
.El
.Ss Verified filesystem image operations
EFI loaders built with loader verification include the following integration
commands.
They do not by themselves establish authenticated kernel root admission.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ic verity-require
Commit to requiring a selected verified image for this loader session.
Call this from authenticated boot policy before loading a kernel or raw preloads.
It rejects a first invocation after any file has been preloaded; repeated
invocations after commitment are harmless.
Until image selection succeeds, executable-source admission and kernel handoff
fail. A failed selection does not clear the requirement and may be retried.
The requirement cannot be cleared through the environment or
.Ic verity-clear ;
returning to an optional-image session requires restarting the loader.
This command is a policy primitive, not automatic startup integration or proof
of authenticated kernel root admission.
.It Ic verity-select Ar image descriptor descriptor-sha256 cache-blocks heap-bytes
Requires a loader built with WITHOUT_LOADER_EFI_TRUST_DB=yes and explicit public
TRUST_ANCHORS for generation manifests. General firmware executable trust alone
is insufficient for root descriptor loading.
The descriptor-sha256 argument is exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits from
the authenticated generation startup policy. Signature-valid descriptor bytes
must match this digest before a device is attached. A digest alone never replaces
manifest authentication. It binds descriptor contents, including geometry, salt
and root, to the selected generation; it is not rollback prevention.
Authenticate the root descriptor through that loader manifest policy and select
its file-backed verified filesystem image as
.Va currdev .
Both memory parameters are explicit nonnegative decimal integers.
The heap budget covers requested device buffers and bookkeeping, not all loader
memory or allocator overhead.
Selection requires no loaded kernel or modules and no existing selection.
The previous current device is retained for release.
While selected, the ELF kernel/module, EFI amd64 Multiboot header and raw-preload
readers require their opened file to belong to the selected verified device.
Explicit paths to another device are rejected, regardless of currdev.
This check adds no whole-file rescan or per-read authorization operation.
For gzip, bzip2, xz and zstd transforms, admission checks the retained backing
file, not the outer device description. The backing file must be a direct UFS
file on the selected device; unsupported source types and transform chains
are rejected in this mode.
EFI Lua readable inputs also require selected-device provenance after selection.
Files on that device use its authenticated block reads instead of an additional
per-file manifest stream verifier. Before selection, existing manifest verification
still applies; ordinary admission success is not proof of a verified source.
This does not change signature or entitlement policy for installed applications.
With WITHOUT_LOADER_VERIEXEC_VECTX=yes, ELF kernels, ELF objects and raw preloads
also use selected-image provenance instead of a redundant per-file manifest scan.
Before selection their existing manifest checks apply. Streaming-verification
builds retain their existing manifest/context behavior; this does not qualify
that configuration for the new disk-image boot path.
.It Ic verity-preload
After loading the kernel, preload the exact descriptor bytes retained during
selection, without reopening the descriptor file.
The selected device must still be current, and an existing root descriptor
preload is rejected.
This command does not assert that all loaded code originated from that device.
Before normal boot or the optional installer calls the kernel exec handler, a selected image
requires exactly one root descriptor of the expected size whose loaded bytes
match the retained descriptor. Missing, duplicate or changed descriptors stop
that boot attempt. This consistency check does not establish release-key scope.
.It Ic verity-clear
After unloading the kernel and modules, restore the previous current device
and release the verified image.
Other open files may keep the image busy; close them before retrying.
If
.Ic verity-require
has committed this session, release is denied even after unloading all files.
.El
.Pp
These operations are not a complete boot protocol.
Kernel root-provider binding and mandatory admission must also be configured
and qualified before using them for a hardened installation.
